body:
So we got some news from the creator of SDL on the issue:
1. Slouken couldn't reproduce the issue with the pedal that was mentioned in the original post, it wasn't detected as a joystick and instead it generated the "B" key press;
2. The webcam goncalo is using does show up as a joystick even in Windows settings, and the fix that will ignore this webcam in the joystick subsystem is now merged in SDL;
3. The webcam reports that it has 256 buttons and some of them are pressed at the startup, so the webcam may have a broken HID descriptor.

If anyone else can get Godot unresponsive when a joystick/gamepad is disconnected, please test [this build](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/issues/108753#issuecomment-3271417773) and post the output here, it would be very helpful!

> I'm getting this error with my 8bitDo Ultimate 2C controller too, version 4.5.beta4.mono.

The same error happens to me. I have an 8BitDo Ultimate 2C Wireless Controller, and when I connect the controller while in any project, whether the project is empty or not, several errors appear in the output. And when I disconnect the controller, Godot stops working and I have to close it through the Task Manager.

I discovered a temporary method to "solve" this problem in Windows 10:

1. Go to "Devices and Printers";
2. Search for 8BitDo Ultimate 2C (if it's not showing up, turn it on);
3. Right-click 8BitDo Ultimate 2C;
4. Click "Properties";
5. Click the "Hardware" tab;
6. Click the "HID-compliant consumer controller device" driver and then click "Properties";
7. Click "Change settings";
8. Click the "Driver" tab;
9. Click "Uninstall Device."

Now, when you open Godot, this problem won't occur. (Just letting you know that I used Google Translate to write this text, so some things may be wrong.)

